1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
In late November twenty eighteen, a heatwave hit

2
00:00:03,200 --> 00:00:06,640
far north Queensland, and Cairns reached over forty-two

3
00:00:06,720 --> 00:00:08,000
degrees.

4
00:00:08,040 --> 00:00:11,840
In about two days, roughly twenty-three thousand spectacled

5
00:00:11,920 --> 00:00:13,920
flying foxes died.

6
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:16,960
These animals pant, lick themselves and fan their

7
00:00:17,040 --> 00:00:18,160
wings to cool down.

8
00:00:18,800 --> 00:00:21,040
So how do animals like them usually keep

9
00:00:21,120 --> 00:00:23,280
their temperature in range, and why did it

10
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:24,240
fail here?

11
00:00:25,467 --> 00:00:30,106
Flying foxes, like kangaroos and humans, are endotherms:

12
00:00:30,187 --> 00:00:32,106
animals that make most of their body heat

13
00:00:32,187 --> 00:00:33,866
from their own metabolism.

14
00:00:34,187 --> 00:00:36,506
They use that heat and control heat loss

15
00:00:36,827 --> 00:00:38,906
to keep core temperature fairly steady.

16
00:00:39,627 --> 00:00:42,586
A lizard is an ectotherm: it gets most

17
00:00:42,627 --> 00:00:44,746
of its heat from its surroundings, and it

18
00:00:44,867 --> 00:00:47,706
still regulates, mainly by moving between sun and

19
00:00:47,867 --> 00:00:48,346
shade.

20
00:00:49,067 --> 00:00:51,386
Endotherms run the feedback loop from the last

21
00:00:51,467 --> 00:00:52,026
video.

22
00:00:53,234 --> 00:00:57,073
When core temperature rises, thermoreceptors in the hypothalamus

23
00:00:57,274 --> 00:01:00,593
detect warmer blood, and skin receptors detect changes

24
00:01:00,674 --> 00:01:01,713
in skin temperature.

25
00:01:02,354 --> 00:01:04,913
The hypothalamus compares this with the set point

26
00:01:05,234 --> 00:01:07,513
and sends nerve impulses to the effectors in

27
00:01:07,554 --> 00:01:08,113
the skin.

28
00:01:08,754 --> 00:01:11,473
Nerve signals are fast and targeted.

29
00:01:11,594 --> 00:01:12,993
That's the neural pathway.

30
00:01:14,200 --> 00:01:18,360
First effector: the skin's arterioles, the small arteries

31
00:01:18,440 --> 00:01:19,080
feeding it.

32
00:01:19,640 --> 00:01:22,200
The smooth muscle in their walls relaxes, so

33
00:01:22,280 --> 00:01:23,120
they widen.

34
00:01:23,320 --> 00:01:24,920
That's vasodilation.

35
00:01:25,480 --> 00:01:28,120
More warm blood flows close to the surface,

36
00:01:28,160 --> 00:01:30,360
the skin warms up, and it loses more

37
00:01:30,440 --> 00:01:31,640
heat to cooler air.

38
00:01:32,440 --> 00:01:34,920
Notice that the blood vessels don't move closer

39
00:01:34,960 --> 00:01:35,760
to the skin.

40
00:01:35,960 --> 00:01:38,840
They widen, so more blood flows through them.

41
00:01:40,067 --> 00:01:43,346
Second effector: sweat glands release sweat onto the

42
00:01:43,427 --> 00:01:44,106
skin.

43
00:01:44,387 --> 00:01:46,706
The cooling comes from evaporation.

44
00:01:47,027 --> 00:01:50,146
Turning water into vapour takes energy, and that

45
00:01:50,387 --> 00:01:52,226
energy comes from the skin and the blood

46
00:01:52,307 --> 00:01:53,506
beneath it.

47
00:01:53,667 --> 00:01:56,866
So in humid air, where sweat evaporates slowly,

48
00:01:56,947 --> 00:01:59,746
sweating cools you far less, even though it's

49
00:01:59,947 --> 00:02:01,026
still pouring out.

50
00:02:02,234 --> 00:02:04,393
In the cold, the loop runs the other

51
00:02:04,514 --> 00:02:06,633
way, as you saw last video.

52
00:02:06,754 --> 00:02:10,553
Arterioles narrow, muscles shiver, and hairs stand up

53
00:02:10,634 --> 00:02:11,913
to trap air.

54
00:02:11,994 --> 00:02:13,433
Hormones help too.

55
00:02:13,514 --> 00:02:16,153
In many mammals, a long cold spell raises

56
00:02:16,234 --> 00:02:18,793
the release of thyroxine, a hormone from the

57
00:02:18,874 --> 00:02:21,593
thyroid gland that raises the metabolic rate of

58
00:02:21,674 --> 00:02:23,993
cells, so they make more heat.

59
00:02:24,074 --> 00:02:26,393
Hormones travel in the blood, so they're slower

60
00:02:26,474 --> 00:02:29,513
than nerves, but their effects last longer.

61
00:02:29,594 --> 00:02:32,153
That's the hormonal pathway, and the two often

62
00:02:32,234 --> 00:02:33,033
work together.

63
00:02:34,234 --> 00:02:36,533
These responses are one of three types of

64
00:02:36,554 --> 00:02:37,353
adaptation.

65
00:02:37,994 --> 00:02:41,753
Physiological adaptations are body processes that switch on

66
00:02:41,834 --> 00:02:44,913
and off, like sweating and vasodilation.

67
00:02:45,194 --> 00:02:48,553
Behavioural adaptations are things the animal does, like

68
00:02:48,714 --> 00:02:50,033
moving into shade.

69
00:02:50,314 --> 00:02:53,753
Structural adaptations are body features, like fur or

70
00:02:53,834 --> 00:02:55,993
a seal's blubber, which are there all the

71
00:02:56,074 --> 00:02:57,673
time, needed or not.

72
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:01,340
The red kangaroo shows all three.

73
00:03:01,620 --> 00:03:03,780
On a hot day, it rests in shade

74
00:03:03,900 --> 00:03:05,140
through the middle of the day.

75
00:03:05,380 --> 00:03:06,540
That's behavioural.

76
00:03:06,820 --> 00:03:09,940
It pants, sweats while hopping, and widens its

77
00:03:10,100 --> 00:03:10,900
skin vessels.

78
00:03:11,220 --> 00:03:12,340
That's physiological.

79
00:03:12,820 --> 00:03:15,460
And it licks its forearms, where a dense

80
00:03:15,540 --> 00:03:18,100
network of blood vessels lies just under thin

81
00:03:18,180 --> 00:03:18,660
skin.

82
00:03:19,140 --> 00:03:21,860
The saliva evaporates and cools the blood there.

83
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:25,220
Licking is a behaviour, the vessel network is

84
00:03:25,300 --> 00:03:28,180
a structure, and the cooling is physiological.

85
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:31,540
Real adaptations often cross the categories.

86
00:03:32,767 --> 00:03:35,526
Across endotherms, there are trends.

87
00:03:35,807 --> 00:03:38,686
Mammals in hot climates often have large, thin

88
00:03:38,847 --> 00:03:41,166
ears or long limbs that shed heat.

89
00:03:41,807 --> 00:03:43,966
Mammals in cold climates tend to be more

90
00:03:44,047 --> 00:03:47,246
compact, with thick fur or blubber, which slows

91
00:03:47,327 --> 00:03:48,366
heat loss.

92
00:03:48,847 --> 00:03:51,806
In a penguin's legs, or a dolphin's flippers,

93
00:03:51,967 --> 00:03:55,086
arteries carrying warm blood out run right beside

94
00:03:55,167 --> 00:03:57,566
veins bringing cool blood back.

95
00:03:57,727 --> 00:04:00,126
Heat passes across to the veins and returns

96
00:04:00,167 --> 00:04:01,166
to the body.

97
00:04:01,247 --> 00:04:03,166
That's countercurrent exchange.

98
00:04:04,367 --> 00:04:06,806
So why did the flying foxes die?

99
00:04:07,087 --> 00:04:09,366
Heat flows from hotter to cooler.

100
00:04:09,647 --> 00:04:11,966
Hotter air added heat instead of carrying it

101
00:04:12,047 --> 00:04:12,726
away.

102
00:04:13,007 --> 00:04:15,566
With little cooling from their surroundings, the flying

103
00:04:15,647 --> 00:04:19,086
foxes relied heavily on evaporation, which uses water

104
00:04:19,167 --> 00:04:20,846
they were already losing.

105
00:04:21,047 --> 00:04:24,286
Negative feedback opposes a change, but each effector

106
00:04:24,367 --> 00:04:25,406
has a limit.

107
00:04:25,487 --> 00:04:27,726
Push past that limit, and the loop keeps

108
00:04:28,007 --> 00:04:30,286
working while body temperature keeps rising.

109
00:04:31,500 --> 00:04:35,060
To keep it: hot means widen and wet.

110
00:04:35,340 --> 00:04:37,580
Cold means narrow and shiver.

111
00:04:38,060 --> 00:04:40,860
And for any adaptation, ask: is it a

112
00:04:40,940 --> 00:04:44,300
body process, a behaviour or a body feature?

113
00:04:45,500 --> 00:04:47,180
Two exam questions.

114
00:04:47,500 --> 00:04:48,220
First.

115
00:04:48,540 --> 00:04:50,780
A cyclist rides on a dry day when

116
00:04:50,820 --> 00:04:52,380
the air is thirty degrees.

117
00:04:52,860 --> 00:04:55,660
Her core temperature rises to thirty-seven point eight

118
00:04:55,820 --> 00:04:58,700
degrees, then holds there while she keeps riding.

119
00:04:59,340 --> 00:05:02,540
Explain how the hypothalamus and two named effectors

120
00:05:02,780 --> 00:05:05,580
stop it rising further, and predict, with a

121
00:05:05,660 --> 00:05:08,220
reason, what would happen on a humid day.

122
00:05:08,940 --> 00:05:10,220
Pause here to answer.

123
00:05:20,634 --> 00:05:23,593
The hypothalamus detects the rise and sends nerve

124
00:05:23,674 --> 00:05:26,873
impulses to skin arterioles and sweat glands.

125
00:05:27,194 --> 00:05:29,833
Wider arterioles bring more warm blood near the

126
00:05:29,914 --> 00:05:32,553
skin, so more heat passes to the cooler

127
00:05:32,634 --> 00:05:33,273
air.

128
00:05:33,594 --> 00:05:36,513
Evaporating sweat takes energy from the skin.

129
00:05:36,794 --> 00:05:39,033
Heat loss now matches the heat her muscles

130
00:05:39,114 --> 00:05:41,753
make, so temperature levels off.

131
00:05:41,874 --> 00:05:44,473
Humid air slows evaporation, so it would rise

132
00:05:44,634 --> 00:05:45,593
higher.

133
00:05:45,634 --> 00:05:47,753
Wet skin alone doesn't cool her.

134
00:05:48,967 --> 00:05:49,526
Second.

135
00:05:50,007 --> 00:05:52,726
In hot weather, koalas move down to lower

136
00:05:52,807 --> 00:05:55,686
branches and press their bodies against the trunk,

137
00:05:55,767 --> 00:05:57,606
which is cooler than their body surface.

138
00:05:58,087 --> 00:05:59,086
They pant.

139
00:05:59,367 --> 00:06:01,526
And their back is covered in dense fur

140
00:06:01,607 --> 00:06:03,286
that reflects part of the sunlight.

141
00:06:03,927 --> 00:06:08,966
Classify each adaptation as behavioural, structural or physiological,

142
00:06:09,047 --> 00:06:11,126
and explain how the first one lowers the

143
00:06:11,207 --> 00:06:12,806
koala's body temperature.

144
00:06:13,127 --> 00:06:14,406
Pause here to answer.

145
00:06:24,834 --> 00:06:28,033
Moving to the trunk is behavioural, panting is

146
00:06:28,114 --> 00:06:30,993
physiological, and the fur is structural.

147
00:06:31,634 --> 00:06:33,793
The trunk is cooler than the koala's body

148
00:06:33,874 --> 00:06:36,753
surface, so heat moves by conduction from its

149
00:06:36,834 --> 00:06:38,113
body into the wood.

150
00:06:38,674 --> 00:06:41,073
That loses heat without using water.

151
00:06:41,714 --> 00:06:44,433
Writing "hugging the tree keeps it cool", with

152
00:06:44,514 --> 00:06:47,313
no heat transfer, is the classic mistake.

153
00:06:47,874 --> 00:06:50,433
Say which way heat moves, and why.

154
00:06:51,634 --> 00:06:54,913
So for any temperature question, name the effector

155
00:06:54,954 --> 00:06:57,713
or feature, say how it changes heat gained

156
00:06:57,794 --> 00:07:00,353
or heat lost, and check its limits.

157
00:07:00,674 --> 00:07:02,913
Next, you'll test heat loss with a model

158
00:07:02,994 --> 00:07:03,793
experiment.

159
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:06,440
Here are the key notes.

160
00:07:06,680 --> 00:07:08,600
Pause the video if you need more time

161
00:07:08,680 --> 00:07:09,480
to copy them.
